REGIONAL REGULATION OF BIMA CITY
NUMBER 6 YEAR 2018
CONCERNING
PROTECTION OF WOMEN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE
BY THE GRACE OF GOD ALMIGHTY
MAYOR OF BIMA,

Considering: a. that every citizen has the right to be free from torture or treatment that degrades human dignity and has the right to obtain security and freedom from all forms of violence;
b. that all forms of violence against women constitute violations of human rights so that their dignity and honor need to be protected and their right to life guaranteed in accordance with their nature and destiny without discrimination;
c. that based on the considerations as referred to in letters a and b, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning Protection of Women Victims of Violence;

In View of: 1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 69 Year 1958 concerning the Establishment of Level II Regions Within Level I Regions of Bali, West Nusa Tenggara and East Nusa Tenggara (State Gazette Year 1958 Number 122, Supplement to State Gazette Number 1655);
3. Law Number 7 Year 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1984 Number 29, Supplement to State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3277);
4. Law Number 39 Year 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1999 Number 165, Supplement to State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia 3886);
5. Law Number 13 Year 2002 concerning Establishment of Bima City in West Nusa Tenggara Province (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2002 Number 26, Supplement to State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4188);
6. Law Number 13 Year 2003 concerning Manpower (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2003 Number 39, Supplement to State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia 4279);
7. Law Number 23 Year 2004 concerning Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2004 Number 95, Supplement to State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia 4419);
8. Law Number 39 Year 2004 concerning Placement and Protection of Indonesian Migrant Workers Abroad (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2004 Number 137, Supplement to State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4447);
9. Law Number 13 Year 2006 concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2006 Number 64, Supplement to State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4636);
10. Law Number 21 Year 2007 concerning Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2007 Number 58, Supplement to State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
11. Law Number 11 Year 2009 concerning Social Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2009 Number 12, Supplement to State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4976);
12. Law Number 12 Year 2011 concerning Establishment of Legislation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2011 Number 82, Supplement to State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5234);
13. Law Number 23 Year 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2014 Number 244, Supplement to State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as amended several times most recently by Law Number 9 Year 2015 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2015 Number 58, Supplement to State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5659);
14. Government Regulation Number 4 Year 2006 concerning Implementation and Cooperation for Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2006 Number 15, Supplement to State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia number 64);
15. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 2 Year 2008 concerning Implementation of Women's Protection;
16. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 01 Year 2010 concerning Minimum Service Standards (SPM) for Integrated Services for Women and Children Victims of Violence (State News of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2010 Number 56);
17. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 5 Year 2010 concerning Guidelines for Establishment and Development of Integrated Service Centers;
18. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 19 Year 2011 concerning Empowerment of Women Victims of Violence;

With Mutual Approval

6.3.text:
Article 3
The objectives of implementing protection for women victims of violence are to: prevent violence against women including human trafficking;
eliminate all forms of violence and exploitation against women;
protect and provide a sense of security for women;
provide services to women victims of violence, reporters, witnesses; and facilitate and mediate household disputes to realize the integrity of a harmonious and prosperous household.
The objectives of implementing protection for women victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), include aspects of: prevention;
services and assistance;
reunification; and empowerment.

Article 13
Regional Government implements efforts to protect women victims of violence through: policies, programs, and activities for the protection of women victims of violence;
establishment of PPT;
ease of service to victims;
pursuing effectiveness and efficiency for the victim recovery process; and cooperation and coordination in victim recovery efforts.
In order to implement the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b, the regional government establishes programs and action activities for the protection of women in 1 (one) Regional Action Plan as a basis for regional apparatus in implementing protection for women victims of violence.
The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2), constitutes part of the Regional Medium-Term Development Plan (RPJMD).
The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2), is further regulated by Mayor Regulation.

Article 2
The implementation of protection for women victims of violence, is carried out based on the principles of: justice;
gender equality; and non-discrimination.

Article 8
Neglect as referred to in Article 4 letter d is caused by: an act that results in the unfulfillment of a woman's needs properly, whether physical, mental, spiritual or social, committed by parents, guardians, or other parties responsible for care;
an act of deliberately neglecting to maintain, care for, or look after a woman as appropriate, committed by parents, guardians or any other party responsible for her care;
an act of neglecting a person within the household environment, whereas according to the law applicable to him or her or due to an agreement or contract he or she is obligated to provide livelihood, care, or maintenance to that person; and/or an act that results in economic dependence by means of restricting and/or prohibiting from engaging in decent work inside or outside the home so that the victim is under the control of that person.

Article 7
Sexual violence as referred to in Article 4 letter c is caused by: acts in the form of sexual harassment;
forced sexual relations;
forced sexual relations in an unnatural or unwanted manner; and/or forced sexual relations with another person for commercial purposes and or specific purposes.

Article 11
Female victims of violence, in addition to obtaining rights as referred to in Article 10, also obtain special rights, as follows: the right to respect and full utilization for survival, growth and development;
the right to basic services;
the right to equal protection;
the right to be free from various stigma; and the right to obtain freedom.

Article 9
Exploitation as referred to in Article 4 letter e is caused by: acts that exploit economically or sexually with the intention of benefiting oneself or others;
acts with or without the consent of the victim which include but are not limited to prostitution, forced labor or services, slavery or similar practices, oppression, extortion, utilization of physical, sexual, reproductive organs, or unlawfully removing or transplanting organs and/or body tissues or utilizing the labor or capabilities of a person by other parties to obtain benefits both material and immaterial; and/or all forms of utilization of sexual organs or other body organs of the victim to obtain benefits, including and not limited to all activities of prostitution or sexual abuse.

